22. The Allahabad High Court was taking a similar view as that taken by the Madras High Court: ILR 25 All 27;
Shahzad Singh v. Ram Ugrah Singh
, AIR 1930 All 211(2);
Abdul Rahman v. Girjesh Bahadur Pal
, AIR 1938 All 235;
Thakur Prasan v. Ram Khelawan
,
AIR 1944 All 240 and Krishna Dei v. Governor-General
in Council, .
But in the Full Bench case of Baijnath v. Ram Bharose, (FB), a contrary view has been taken, which adopted the reasoning of the Full Bench decision in AIR 1940 Pat 346 (FB) holding that whether an appeal has or has not abated as a whole will depend upon considerations other than Order 41 Rule 4.

The High Courts of Assam, Madhya Pradesh Rajasthan and Orissa have also taken the same view as that of the Full Bench of the Patna High Court in AIR 1940 Fat 346 (FB); Sonahar Ali v. Mukbul Ali, AIR 1956 Assam 164;
Pyarelal v. Modi Sikharchand
, ;
Reghu Sutar v. Nrusingha Nath
, ;
Padmaram v. Surja
, and
Ghaki Mal Hukam Chand v. Punjab National Bank
, . In , J., sitting singly, has considered the matter in great detail and has accepted the view of the Patna High Court in the Full Bench case. In the decision turned on the question of the representation of the estate by the eldest son of a Hindu joint family-firm who, on the death of the father, became automatically the manager or karta of the firm.
